---
import Admin from '../../../layouts/Admin.astro';
---
<Admin title="Photos" active="photos">

  <h1 class="adm-h1">Photos</h1>
  <p class="adm-lede">
    Photographs of finished tools, work in progress and the shop floor, for the
    Our Work section on the homepage. Adding one commits it to the website;
    it appears about two minutes later.
  </p>

  <p class="ph-status" id="ph-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>

  <p class="ph-loading" id="ph-loading">Loading the gallery…</p>

  <div id="ph-panel" hidden>

    <!-- ── add ── -->
    <section class="ph-add">
      <h2 class="ph-h2">Add photographs</h2>

      <div class="ph-drop" id="ph-drop">
        <label class="ph-choose">
          <input type="file" id="ph-file" accept="image/*" multiple hidden />
          <span class="ph-choose-btn">Choose photographs</span>
        </label>
        <p class="ph-drop-note">
          or drag them here · up to 8 at a time · straight from a phone is fine,
          they are shrunk before they are sent
        </p>
      </div>

      <div id="ph-queue" hidden></div>
      <p class="ph-hint" id="ph-hint" hidden></p>
      <button type="button" class="ph-btn" id="ph-upload" hidden disabled>Add</button>

      <!-- The naming rules are enforced by the API, not here. This says why,
           before someone types "press tool chennai press tool" and is refused
           by a machine with no explanation. -->
      <div class="ph-why">
        <h3>Why the two boxes matter</h3>
        <p>
          <b>What it is</b> becomes the photograph's filename, the caption under
          it, and part of what Google reads. <b>Blanking die for 3 mm mild
          steel</b> is findable. <b>IMG_4471</b> is not.
        </p>
        <p>
          <b>The description</b> is read aloud to blind visitors and is the main
          thing Google Images has to go on. Say what is visible — the material,
          the operation, the machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Write both as you would say them to a customer. Repeating keywords —
          <i>press tool chennai press tool manufacturer press tool</i> — is the
          one thing that actively damages a site's ranking, so the panel refuses
          it rather than letting it through.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── the existing gallery ── -->
    <section class="ph-have">
      <div class="ph-have-head">
        <div>
          <h2 class="ph-h2">On the homepage</h2>
          <p class="ph-count" id="ph-count"></p>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="ph-btn" id="ph-save" disabled>Saved</button>
      </div>
      <p class="ph-order-note">
        The <b>main photo</b> also appears beside the About text. Order here is
        the order on the homepage.
      </p>
      <div id="ph-list"></div>
    </section>

  </div>

  <section class="adm-brief">
    <h2>What to photograph</h2>
    <p class="adm-brief-lede">
      Around twenty is right. A tight set of real work reads as confidence; a
      hundred snapshots reads as filler. Daylight, no styling, a phone is fine.
    </p>
    <ul class="adm-list">
      <li><b>Finished tools on the bench</b> — the thing a customer is buying. The most important shot on this list.</li>
      <li><b>A die set open</b>, showing the punches and the die block. Engineers look at this and can tell whether you know what you are doing.</li>
      <li><b>Something mounted in a press</b>, ideally mid-job.</li>
      <li><b>The wire-cut or the jig borer running.</b> Machines with work in them, not machines standing idle.</li>
      <li><b>A jig or fixture with the component sitting in it.</b> Shows the fit, which is the whole point of a fixture.</li>
      <li><b>The shop floor, wide.</b> One honest photograph of the room.</li>
      <li><b>Your team working.</b> People buy from people. This matters more than it sounds.</li>
      <li><b>Inspection</b> — a gauge or a height master in use.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="adm-brief-foot">
      Avoid: anything under a customer NDA, anything with a drawing number or
      part name visible that is not ours to show, and anything blurry. A blurry
      photograph of good work does the work a disservice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    import '../../../scripts/admin-photos.js';
  </script>

</Admin>
